    "narrative": "The Austin Waves offensive engine is anchored today by **Gunnar Henderson**, who earns our highest efficiency score of 83.29. Facing Elder, **Gunnar Henderson** benefits from a massive 16% park boost and a favorable platoon matchup, positioning him as the primary engine for power and run production. Similarly, **Jacob Wilson** (74.08) and **Drake Baldwin** (72.68) represent top-tier starts, with both leveraging elite SP skill advantages and prime #1 spots in their respective lineups. **Drake Baldwin** remains particularly intriguing, as his 16% park edge and high xwOBA metrics make him the most efficient battery option on the board despite the challenge of Young.\n\nDepth management remains critical, highlighted by the decision to bench **Kyle Tucker** (51.31) in favor of higher-floor daily plays. While **Kyle Tucker** possesses immense upside, his matchup against McLean is stifled by a difficult 3.43 xERA opponent and a suboptimal #7 spot in the order, pushing him behind the immediate efficiency of **Dominic Smith** and **Cole Carrigg**. **Jackson Merrill** (59.99) sits right on the borderline; however, his pairing with a favorable SP delivery profile that projects well for our scarcity-driven focus on stolen bases keeps him in the lineup over the surging **Brayan Rocchio** (54.72).\n\nWe are tracking several situational variables across the roster. **Nasim Nunez** (61.57) and **Curtis Mead** (57.04) are tasked with navigating Bratt, where the SP skill profile is favorable, though the lower batting order for **Nasim Nunez** acts as a drag on his overall output. While the lineup appears stable, we remain wary of the 4.88 xERA matchup for **Miguel Vargas**, who is nonetheless starting due to an elite 0.392 xwOBA projection. Owners should monitor weather reports for all venues, but for now, the Waves are optimized to maximize daily efficiency by prioritizing hitters with distinct park and platoon advantages over raw seasonal averages.",
